  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{4}(6{x}^{-1/2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(6{x}^{-1/2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2{x}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =12{x}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =12({4}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{3}({4}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =12(2-1)+\\frac{1}{3}(64-1) \\\\  & =12+21 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
